=== layout: one-panel

# Predict Independent Doubles

@body

[*Step 2: Multiply Adjacent Probabilities*]{color=#B74300}

{table-style=working}
| Interval | Percent | Probability |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A-J | 16% | 0.16 |
| J-E | 25% | 0.25 |

$$
P(\mathrm{DCO})_{\mathrm{independent}} = 0.16 \times 0.25 = 0.04
$$

Independence predicts that *4%* of all offspring will be DCO.

=== layout: one-panel

# Find the Expected Count

@body

[*Step 3: Multiply by All Offspring*]{color=#007A00}

$$
E_{\mathrm{DCO}} = 0.04 \times 7000 = 280
$$

{table-style=working}
| DCO Count | Offspring |
| --- | --- |
| Observed | 70 |
| Expected under independence | 280 |

Which count belongs in the denominator for coincidence?

=== layout: one-panel

# Check Coincidence

@body

[*Step 4: Divide Observed by Expected*]{color=#003FFF}

$$
\mathrm{CoC} = \frac{70}{280} = \frac{1}{4} = 0.25
$$

We observed *25% of the expected doubles*.

Is 25% also the interference?

=== layout: one-panel

# Check Interference

@body

[*Step 5: Subtract from One*]{color=#A719DB}

$$
I = 1 - 0.25 = 0.75 = \frac{3}{4} = 75\%
$$

- *25%* is the coincidence: the fraction of expected doubles observed.
- *75%* is the interference: the reduction relative to the expectation.
- For percentage interference, enter *75*.

=== layout: one-panel

# Compare the Two Examples

@body

{table-style=comparison}
| Quantity | F-W-P | A-J-E |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Observed DCO | 54 | 70 |
| Expected DCO | 67.5 | 280 |
| Coincidence | 80% | 25% |
| Interference | 20% | 75% |

In each example, coincidence and interference add to *100%*.
A smaller fraction of expected doubles occurs in the A-J-E cross.

